   "source": [
    "# 04 · Secondary analysis – ASCA at protein level\n",
    "\n",
   ### How the unbalanced design is handled

The design is **unbalanced**: the 28 conditions are spread unevenly over 6 MS batches (2 to 7 conditions each), and every batch covers a different growth-rate range.

| batch | conditions | growth rate (h⁻¹) |
|---|---|---|
| Wu Group #1 | 6 | 0.96 – 2.19 |
| Wu Group #2 | 2 | 0.95 – 1.45 |
| Wu Group #3 | 5 | 0.67 – 1.45 |
| Zhu 2025 | 6 | 0.42 – 1.90 |
| Mori 2021 | 7 | 0.49 – 1.12 |
| Schmidt 2016 | 2 | 1.03 – 2.30 |

Classical ASCA assumes a balanced design, in which the effect matrices are orthogonal automatically. Here batch and growth rate overlap (a batch with only fast conditions also has its own platform offset), so the split of variance between them is not unique. `asca.run_asca` handles this explicitly:

1. **Replicates are averaged first** (section 1), so a batch with many replicate runs does not weigh more than a batch with few.
2. **Growth rate is a continuous covariate (1 df)**, not binned into levels, so there are no empty or sparsely filled design cells.
3. **Sequential (type I) sums of squares, batch first:** `X_c = X_batch + X_growth|batch + X_batch×growth + E`. Growth is fitted only after the batch means are removed, so it is estimated from **within-batch contrasts**. Variation that batch and growth rate share is credited to batch: the conservative choice, because entering growth first would credit growth with the platform offsets between studies. The effect matrices are made orthogonal by projection, and the code stops if they are not.
4. **A batch × growth-rate interaction** tests whether the batches agree on each protein's slope. Batches with a single condition, or with no spread in growth rate, add nothing to it.
5. **Restricted permutation tests:** growth rates are shuffled only **within** each batch (the interaction uses a Freedman–Lane test with a pseudo-F statistic), so every permuted dataset has exactly the same imbalance as the real one, and the p-values are valid for this design.

**Consequence:** each batch contributes to the shared slope in proportion to the spread of growth rates it covers. Schmidt 2016 and Wu Group #2, with two conditions each, contribute one contrast each.
    "**Model.** `X = X_batch + X_growth + X_batch×growth + E`, fitted in that order (sequential sums of squares):\n",
    "\n",
    "| term | meaning |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| `X_batch` | one mean level per batch (platform / laboratory offset) |\n",
    "| `X_growth` | one growth-rate slope per protein, **shared by all batches** |\n",
    "| `X_batch×growth` | how far each batch's own slope departs from the shared slope |\n",
    "| `E` | residual |\n",
    "\n",
    "The interaction tests the key assumption of the project: that the studies agree on how the proteome changes with growth rate.'\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**Preprocessing, in this order (all parameters in `config.yml`)**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. in-scope samples only;\n",
    "2. keep proteins quantified in every in-scope sample of **every** dataset (logical AND). The matrix is then complete and **no value is imputed**. The earlier OR rule kept proteins that one platform never measured and then filled whole columns with half-minimum values;\n",
    "3. log10;\n",
    "4. average replicates to conditions;\n",
    "5. column mean-centring – last, on the final set of rows.\n",
    "\n",
    "The detection filter never looks at growth rate, and ASCA is descriptive, so there is no label leakage. The price is a smaller protein set, reported below."
   ]
  },
